176   Event Types Requiring Continuous Monitoring
177    NIST 800-53 Field: AU-2(c) and (d), AU-12(3)
178    All of the event types listed above are logged. However, Dispel recommends logs of the following
179    event types: login events, data and permission changes, and remote access connections made to target
180    assets should be audited continually (ie: at the time the event log is generated) by Customers.
181    Together, tracking these events will ensure a Customer will be aware of:
182
183    1. Who is accessing the system or if someone is trying to break into the system.
184    2. If someone is going to get to do something different than what they were previously approved to
185       do.
186    3. What a person could do to systems that someone is using the Dispel Zero Trust Engine to reach.
187
188    A person must be authenticated in order to manipulate permissions, update dashboard data, alter
189    infrastructure, or gain access to a virtual desktop through which they can connect to a target asset.
190    In addition to platform logging which monitors all of the above critical actions, each virtual desktop
191    is subject to screen recording, traffic monitoring, and event logging. The subset of screen recording,
192    login events, permission changes, and connections made to external assets should be, in Dispel’s
193    opinion, sufficient to support after-the-fact investigations.
194
195    The Customer shall be responsible for decisions related to the controls mapped to this section.

