300   <FedRAMP Specific> Protection of Audit Information
301    NIST 800-53 Field: AU-9, AU-9(2), and AU-9(3), AU-9(4)
302    Any audit data held within the Dispel Zero Trust Engine is protected within the Dispel system using
303    the same access controls, cryptographic mechanisms, and alerts as any other system components
304    within the system boundary. Audit data is stored in a physically different location from audited
305    systems. Access to audit records is limited to Platform Managers with a Security Tier role.
306
307    Audit information, at the Customer’s discretion, can be pushed out of the Dispel Zero Trust Engine
308    system to a Customer audit system. If that takes place, the responsibility of protecting that
309    information passes to the Customer.
310
311    Access, modification, or deletion event Alerts generated by a Log Server component will go to
312    Dispel System Administrators unless the Customer has deactivated those alerts. If the Customer
313    deactivates alerts to Dispel System Administrators or configures there Dispel Zero Trust Engine
314    deployment to forward audit data to a Customer system, the Customer is responsible for alerting the
315    proper personnel or roles in the event of unauthorized access, modification, and deletion.
316
317    It is the Customer’s responsibility to configure which individuals may access that log server, as well
318    as the permissions those users have once on the log server.

319   <FedRAMP> Non-Repudiation of Audit System Adjustments
320    NIST 800-53 Field: AU-10
321    All application actions within the DZTE are logged and audit records provide their authentication
322    action as evidence of non-repudiation, which can be seen through Dashboard audit logs.
323
324
325    The Development Operations Lead shall be responsible for enabling these capabilities.
326
327    Across all components of Dispel Zero Trust Engine deployments, access to any system requires an
328    irrefutable authentication step through a strong log in and second factor of authorization. From that
329    moment, any action within a Dispel Zero Trust Engine component – including connections from a
330    Virtual Desktop to a Log Server component – is monitored and tagged to that user. As each action –
331    including addition, modification, deletion, approval, sending, or receiving of data – is tagged to a
332    user after their multi-factor authentication step, and all actions on the Log Server are recorded, the
333    actions performed on log servers are tied irrefutably to the user-id.

